<title>LUXEMBOURG: Court suspends payment to fund social programmes.</title>
<headline>Court suspends payment to fund social programmes.</headline>
<dateline>LUXEMBOURG 1996-09-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Court of Justice, responding to bids from Britain and Germany, stopped funding on Tuesday for pilot social projects for the poor and elderly agreed by the European Commission.</p>
<p>Britain and Germany argued the decision should have been taken by the Council of Ministers, not the Commission. The court noted similarities between a decision challenged and proposals which the Council did not adopt and decided further review is warranted.</p>
<p>The Commission had approved approximately 12 million ECU for certain pilot projects to assist the elderly and to combat poverty and social exclusion.</p>
<p>The President of the Court issued interim rulings in Cases C-239/96R and C-240/96R. The full judgment will be issued at a later date and the court is in no way bound by the interim ruling.</p>
</text>
